# No Thread Spawning in High-Frequency Callbacks

Never use `std::thread::spawn` inside a callback that fires on every
filesystem event, network packet, or timer tick.

## What went wrong

The pipeline cache's `notify` watcher callback spawned a new OS thread on
every filesystem modify/create/remove event, just to set a boolean flag.
Under heavy churn, this created hundreds of short-lived threads — an
unnecessary resource drain.

## Rule

1. If you only need to flip a flag, use `AtomicBool` with `Ordering::Relaxed`.
2. If you need to send structured data, use a bounded channel
   (`std::sync::mpsc` or `crossbeam`).
3. If you absolutely need async from a sync callback, use
   `tokio::runtime::Handle::try_current()` with `spawn` — not
   `std::thread::spawn` + `block_on`.

## Example

```rust
// BAD: thread per event
std::thread::spawn(move || {
    rt.block_on(async { state.write().await.dirty = true; });
});

// GOOD: lock-free atomic flag
dirty_flag.store(true, Ordering::Relaxed);
```
